<h1>associative</h1>
<p>
(definition)
</p>

<p>
<strong>Definition:</strong>
A function where f(A, f(B, C)) = f(f(A, B), C).
</p>

<p>
<strong>See also</strong>
<a href="commutative.html"><em>commutative</em></a>.
</p>

<p>
<em>Note:
Multiplication is associative, e.g., 2 &times; (3 &times; 4) =  (2 &times; 3) &times; 4, and commutative, e.g., 2 &times; 3 = 3 &times; 2. Subtraction is neither associative, e.g., 2 - (3 - 4) &ne; (2 - 3) - 4, nor commutative, e.g., 2 - 3 &ne; 3 - 2.  Because of rounding floating point addition on a computer is not associative, e.g., (1000000 + .00001) + .00001 &ne; 1000000 + (.00001 + .00001) (actual values depend on the details of the computer addition), but is commutative, e.g., 1000000 + .00001 = .00001 + 1000000.  <br /> Cartesian product is associative, e.g., {1, 2} &times; ({a, b} &times; {X, Y}) = ({1, 2} &times; {a, b}) &times; {X, Y}, but is not commutative, e.g., {1, 2} &times; {a, b} &ne; {a, b} &times; {1, 2}.</em>
</p>

<p>Author: <a href="../Other/contrib.html#authorPEB">PEB</a></p>

